{%- comment -%}
  robots.txt.liquid
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The live theme has NO robots.txt.liquid, so celestelamour.com currently
  serves Shopify's stock robots.txt. That default is sensible but knows
  nothing about the two things this store actually does: Boost PFS faceted
  filtering, which can generate a very large number of parameterised
  collection URLs, and a set of utility pages that exist for customers but
  have no business in the index.

  WHERE IT GOES
  templates/robots.txt.liquid in the live theme. Shopify renders this in
  place of the default. Adding the file at all is low risk as long as the
  `robots.default_groups` loop below is preserved: that loop reproduces
  Shopify's own rules, so this file only ever ADDS to them.

  READ THIS BEFORE USING IT TO REMOVE ANYTHING
  robots.txt controls CRAWLING, not INDEXING. A URL that is already indexed
  and then gets disallowed here does not drop out of Google - it can stay in
  the index, typically as a bare URL with no snippet, because Google can no
  longer fetch it to see the noindex. So:

    - To keep Google OUT of a section that is not yet indexed -> robots.txt.
    - To REMOVE something already indexed -> let it stay crawlable and serve
      either a `noindex` meta tag (for pages you are keeping) or HTTP 410
      (for pages you are deleting). Only add a robots.txt rule afterwards,
      once the URLs have actually dropped out.

  That distinction matters specifically for the 57 scraped blog articles. Do
  NOT disallow /blogs/verbs-list/ here as a first move: delete the articles so
  the URLs return 410, let Google recrawl and drop them, and only then
  consider a rule.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% for group in robots.default_groups %}
{{- group.user_agent }}
{% for rule in group.rules %}{{ rule }}
{% endfor %}
{%- if group.user_agent.value == '*' -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Faceted-filter URLs.

  IMPORTANT: the live theme uses NATIVE Shopify filtering, not Boost PFS.
  Boost (bc-sf-filter) is installed at the shop level — its settings are in the
  shop metafields — but the live theme does not render it. What the live theme
  actually renders is `snippets/gs-plp-filter.liquid`, which iterates
  `filter.values` and emits `<input type="checkbox" name="{{ v.param_name }}">`,
  producing native parameters: `filter.v.option.*`, `filter.p.tag` and
  `filter.v.price.gte` / `.lte`. And `snippets/collection-filters-facets.liquid`
  emits real crawlable anchors (`<a href="{{ value.url_to_remove }}">`), so
  these are not JS-only states — Google can and does follow them.

  Every combination of colour, size and price is therefore a distinct crawlable
  URL returning a near-duplicate of the parent collection. Across 90
  collections that multiplies into far more URLs than the store has real pages,
  and crawl effort spent there is effort not spent on the collection pages you
  want surfaced as sitelinks.

  The `pf_*` lines are kept as a defensive measure only, in case Boost is ever
  switched on in the live theme.
{%- endcomment -%}
Disallow: /*?filter.
Disallow: /*&filter.
Disallow: /*?sort_by
Disallow: /*&sort_by
Disallow: /*?pf_
Disallow: /*&pf_

{%- comment -%}
  Tag-path URLs. `snippets/filter-by-tags.liquid` and
  `snippets/selected-tags-filter.liquid` generate paths of the form
  /collections/leggings/black — a separate URL per tag, per collection, each a
  thin subset of its parent.

  The Allow line below matters and must stay. Shopify also serves products at
  /collections/{collection}/products/{product}, which matches the same pattern.
  Those are duplicates of /products/{handle} and Shopify canonicals them
  correctly — but only if Google can still fetch them to read the canonical
  tag. Blocking them would strand the canonical signal. Google honours the more
  specific rule, so the Allow wins for product paths and the Disallow applies
  to tag paths.
{%- endcomment -%}
Allow: /collections/*/products/
Disallow: /collections/*/*

{%- comment -%}
  Session, tracking and referral parameters. Judge.me appends
  ?jdgm_referral_location=...&utm_source=judgeme to product links, which
  creates a duplicate of every product URL it touches.
{%- endcomment -%}
Disallow: /*?jdgm_referral_location*
Disallow: /*&jdgm_referral_location*
Disallow: /*?variant=*
Disallow: /*&variant=*

{%- comment -%}
  Utility pages. These are real pages customers use, but they carry no
  information a searcher wants and they dilute the set of pages Google can
  choose from when it builds a sitelinks block.

  NOTE: if any of these is already indexed, add a noindex meta tag to its
  template FIRST and wait for it to drop, then keep these lines. Adding the
  rule alone will freeze it in the index.
{%- endcomment -%}
Disallow: /pages/survey
Disallow: /pages/review-submission
Disallow: /pages/account
Disallow: /pages/track-order
Disallow: /pages/data-sharing-opt-out
Disallow: /pages/workflow-dashboard
Disallow: /account
Disallow: /cart
Disallow: /checkouts
Disallow: /tools/

{%- comment -%}
  Theme previews. This store has 19 themes, 18 of them unpublished. Preview
  URLs of the form /?preview_theme_id=NNN or /t/NN/... render a full copy of
  the storefront and are a classic accidental duplicate-content source if one
  ever gets linked to externally.
{%- endcomment -%}
Disallow: /*?preview_theme_id*
Disallow: /*&preview_theme_id*
Disallow: /*?_ab=0*
Disallow: /*?_fd=0*
{%- endif -%}

{%- if group.sitemap != blank -%}
{{ group.sitemap }}
{%- endif -%}
{% endfor %}
